messages "uglymouth**To: redsexySubj: coding answers (I don't waste too much time either)Cc: uglymouthDate: Nov 231) use the time() efun to get the local Linz time in a seconds value.Adjust this for timezone with time difference with Austria * 3600. Usethe efun ctime( adjusted_time_value ) to get the time returned as a string.The biggest problem is that we don't have logged who is from which timezone.So you could either build a huge database wherein you link sites withtimezones (note this needs continuous upgrading!!) or you could have playersinput their own timezone somewhere along the line.2) the basic idea is to set into the room leading to the next world a typeinteger (or better even a type status) variable monster_dead. Add a function:void set_monster_dead( int i ) {}Then check for if the move is allowed by using the move() function as iscoded in /room/room.c. I assumed the exit was 'enter'. The rest of the codeis a pretty standard way to handle move checks. If unclear, just ask :-)This would be the neatest way, but you might as well add an action enter,which uses the same check for monster_dead, if you like.int move( string str ) {  if( !str ) str = query_verb()  if( str == enter && monster_dead ) {    notify_fail( You cannot go that way right now.\\n )    return 0  }  return ::move( str )}Next, in the monster you need to add two things as well. At creation timeyou need a call to the room, to allow the entrance to be used again:void reset( int arg ) {  ...other reset code...  call_other( room_file, set_monster_dead, 0 )}Then there is a function monster_died that automatically gets called when themonster dies. It must return 0 for the monster to actually die (if non-zero,the monster stays alive. can be used for some neat features as well):int monster_died() {  call_other( room_file, set_monster_dead, 1 )  return 0}3) There are basically two ways of doing this:A) In the monster_died() function you check all players online for thefile_name of the room they are in. If you have the area in a separatedirectory, you could check for that path in the room's file name and ata match move the player back.B) When entering or leaving the area, you add/remove the player's nameto/from a global variable (i.e. string *entered;) in the room. Then adda function that moves the players stored in the variable back to the entryroom. Then add to monster_died() in the monster code a call to thisfunction.For either case consider the possibilities of teleports. If the area isnot entirely non-teleport, players may return there even after the monsterdied.Hopefully these remarks prove a bit useful to you.All the best,Uglymouth**sauron**To: redsexySubj: commentsDate: Nov 24Disclaimer: Please do not be personally offended by what you are about to            read... It is not a personal attack on you... just your            coding. ;D I do not pull punches.Ok, lets start with general coding principles... :)I don't know who's code you copied when you started coding, but I reallywish you'd asked either Mangla or Jez or myself for a decent examplebefore you did...The first rule of coding is indentation. This is very important for anyonewho is going to (attempt to) read your code now or in the future. Properand consistent indentation helps the reader to follow the functions, helpyou to keep track of things like curly braces for functions and ifstatements, and hence helps you debug swiftly and painlessly. To this end,take a look in /players/sauron/redsexy/ at the two files I indented. Mygeneral rule is to indent 4 spaces whenever you open another { but you canindent anything from 2 - 4 as you see fit.  Spacing is also important to  make the code clearer for people to read.My suggestion would be to play around with different styles and see whatis most comfortable for you to work with. Some ppl like to space   everything out. Others like to put ( )  around everything to separate things. The driver will ignore spaces, and ( ) as long as they 'balance' so it's up to you. I have used my personal preference in the 2 files Iindented, so you can look at those and see what you think. My only otherpiece of advice on this would be: don't take things to extremes. Too much(or too little) indentation/spacing can make code just as hard to read ascode which is inconsistently indented/spaced.The second thing you will note is that the functions have extra words inthem. For example:reset(arg) {has becomevoid reset(int arg) {This is called strict typing, and this also helps anyone reading your codeto understand what is going on. In LPC, every function and every variablehas a type...The type of a variable is the type of value it holds. The type of afunction is the type of value it returns. If a function doesn't returnanything, you can set it's return type to 'void' (and then use statementslike \"if(blah) return;\".In principle, when creating your own functions, you can make these typesanything you wish.  However, when working with predefined mud functions,you have to follow mud rules. For example, the function reset() takes aninteger as an argument, and no return value is expected.Hence the above definition. Similarly, init()  doesn't have any arguments,and no return value is expected. There are other mud defined functions(such as exit() which is called when you exit a room, and like init()takes no arguments and returns no value) but reset() and init() are themain two.The other area where there are fixed rules regarding the types for afunction is when creating functions for actions declared usingadd_action(). In this case, the single argument to the function is always a string, and the return type should always be an integer - 1 for a   successful action, and 0 for a failure.You can specify a failure message using notify_fail() if you so wish. Inthat case, if the command fails totally, the msg will be displayed(assuming that no other notify_fail msg is specified in subsiquentcommands that match the cmd entered by the player). Note that if youreturn 1 from the function, it is considered to have succeeded, and hencethe notify_fail msg will not be used.Above all, you should be consistent. You should not return 2 or moredifferent types from the same function (unless you mean to of course ;).Hence, using your dig function as an example, if the argument doesn'tmatch, simply return 0.Ok, now onto specifics...I'm not sure what you were trying to do with the dig function in theinherit room, but it confused the hell out of me!  There must be a nicerway to do whatever it is you want to do. Do you ever plan to have that cmddo anything useful, or is it just a 'goof-off'? :)  If you want it to beuseful, I can come up with a good system for you. If it's just a goofy    cmd, I can still come up with a good system... :)  Messing around withyour argument value like that isn't generally a good thing... you losetrack of what the command giver actually typed (a piece of data which issometimes very useful), and it can get kinda confusing.I only took a look at one of the actual rooms - I couldn't face the rest.;D The descriptions are fine... I'm not going to comment on those at thistime. Once again, the above suggestions apply... indentation, spacing,   strict typing. I've also added a useful trick with dealing with longstrings in code... As you can see, you can 'wrap' a string onto a new lineusing the '\\' character... This avoids string addition, and makes the codeflow better when being read. It also means that you can see how the finallong_desc will turn out. I saw that you like to have 2 spaces after theend of a sentence. Personally I LOATHE that convention, but if you want itthat way, that's up to you. :)My only other request/suggestion would be to make a map of the area sothat ppl who test the area for you can refer to it for things like  checking exits, and finding their way around. Soon, I intend to make it arequirement for area approval that a map is present in the area dir for   reference, so it's a good idea to get used to it. :)General suggestions...Ok, I always find it a good idea to separate the code for each area in  your directory. This may not seem important now, but when you have 5areas, and the monsters for all of them are in /players/redsexy/monster/you'll realise what i'm talking about. :) The easiest way is to create   subdirs in each area dir. In this case, you created/players/redsexy/areas/island/. From there you could have a rooms subdir for rooms (everything that is in /players/redsexy/areas/island now), amonsters subdir (for whatever is in /players/redsexy/monsters) and anobjects subdir (for whatever is in /players/redsexy/objects). You may wishto create a secure subdir for 'sensitive' information such as questsolutions or just info that you want to keep from the prying eyes of   wizards like the map for a maze where players can find good items. I wouldalso suggest limiting the number of such subdirs... For example, there isno need to create armour and weapons subdirs (IMHO) - everything can go inthe general 'objects' subdir. Once again, it's a personal preference. I see that you have a defs.h file. I also see that you copied it fromMangla's dir and simply changed the paths... :) If you don't understand   how they work, ask and I will explain them to you. They can be veryhelpful if you use certain phrases alot in your code.Hmm... have I said enough for now? I think so - you have alot to digestthere. If you have questions, please feel free to ask. Sometime soon I  hope to get some decent examples written for my mentees, so I'll let youhave a copy of them as well (assuming that I get done before you finish your area :). I am also creating a general MuD/LPC web page, but I only  started that last week, so it's not got very far yet.Have a nice day... :) I'll be on later today.Dave**saffrin**To: redsexySubj: addressDate: Dec 17Cindi Cardinale/Mark Wheeler15 Rollins AvePearl River, NY 10965USA:)OkayCin**brainsprain**To: redsexySubj: I miss youDate: Jan  1Where are you girl, i never see you around anymore, i miss youam i cast off as so much flotsam and jetsam???Stiletto (err Brainsprain)**brainsprain**To: redsexySubj: my addressDate: Apr  8My school addressEric Tucker3468 Freedom QuadUniversity At Albany1400 Washington Ave.Albany NY, 12222at home (summer)Eric Tucker173 North Rd.Fort Ann, NY 12827**redlego**To: redsexySubj: whats up???Date: Jul 28HEY YOU, whats goin on??????@!!!!?!??!????!!?!?**doolotz**To: redsexySubj: addieCc: qDate: Aug 29130.89.227.77 3400ordanceable.student.utwente.nl 3400Doo**paris**To: redsexyRe:   Hi hiDate: Sep  2Whilst still on the subject, here's my numbers *laugh*Mobile: 0973 326078Home:   0181 922 6935ttfnmike**brainsprain**To: redsexySubj: i am backDate: Sep 11I am back, where are you, would like to talk to you.May have discovered some things of interest to you.Tuck**gareth**To: redsexySubj: Heya!Date: Sep 26Nice plan! - hehehehe I'm a libra you know - heheheheheh.Gar.**tatsuo**To: manglaSubj: summonreCc: redsexy, wilbieDate: Mar  7I found the file that could possibly crash the mud in the summonersIt can be easily removedscontrol.c in the obj dirremoving the seeker is no big dealI can't find any other reason to to reopen the guild.I will bin it up and remove the seeker, and that should be good to goRedsexy or wilbie have bothed voiced interest in running that guild.2.Barb guild moved back to original location.3.  Conan moved near campfire, is valid mob4.  You should move your jedi guild inside redsexy's area. it rocks andis very jedi themeperhaps mos eisley5. have a nice day.**llllllisa**To: redsexySubj: Summoners GuildCc: llllllisaDate: Ma